triage: fix_tests: All four scenarios crash before any rule is checked, because the test `given` blocks set `round`, `current_seat`, `top_a`, `size_a`, `ox_a` and similar names. Those are internal bookkeeping the coder was free to name and organise, not part of the interface, so the failures say nothing about the game's rule logic.
rewriting the independent tests
step D: evaluating
triage: fix_game: The R2 test is correct, and the game breaks two rules. Seat 1 held 7 against tops 11 and 25 with N = 30; (7-11) mod 30 = 26 and (7-25) mod 30 = 12, so nothing fits and seat 1 must take, yet the game let it place '7 on A'. Seat 2 then took Pile B [25] and was charged 1 ox instead of 2, which points to an off-by-one in the ox lookup.
